# Model delegation - ring-fencing top-tier tokens

Claude Code twin: `.claude/rules/model-delegation.md` (canonical - includes the proven token
ratio and the full skeleton brief). Claude Code sessions have `builder` and `recon` subagents
pinned to Sonnet in `.claude/agents/`.

## The split (applies to Cursor model choice too)

- **Opus 5 - the default top tier**: architecture decisions, writing briefs/specs, seam +
  security review, debugging weird failures, deploy go/no-go. Not typing implementations.
- **Fable 5 - named exception only**: genuinely frontier reasoning, or debugging that already
  defeated Opus 5. It is ~2x Opus 5's price ($10/$50 per MTok vs $5/$25), so reach for it by
  naming the reason, never by habit. If the reason is "this is hard", try higher effort on
  Opus 5 first.
- **Sonnet 5 - mid tier**: implementation from a self-contained brief, fan-out recon,
  docs from a spec. Runs its own verification before reporting done.
- **Small models**: mechanical transforms only - below the bar for this repo's conventions.

## Effort before tier

Raise or lower the effort setting before switching models - it is cheaper than a tier switch and
does not throw away the prompt cache. Low for recon and mechanical work, high/xhigh for coding and
agentic work, max only when correctness beats cost. Effort cannot fix a price tier.

## Protect the prompt cache

Cache reads cost ~0.1x base input; cache writes cost 1.25x-2x. Continue sessions rather than
restarting them, do not switch models mid-session (caches are model-scoped), and keep the early
part of the prompt frozen - editing a rules file mid-session invalidates everything after it.
On a top-tier model, wasted cache writes are routinely the largest line on the bill.

Default working sessions to a Sonnet-class model; escalate deliberately for design, security,
cross-component work, and stuck debugging.

## Brief anatomy (all six, every delegation - human or agent)

1. Context (3-5 sentences, incl. parallel workstreams)
2. Read-first list - exact files AND line ranges, nothing open-ended
3. FIXED contracts between workstreams, marked "do not renegotiate"
4. Deliverables with acceptance criteria
5. Verify-before-finishing commands (tests, type-check, ASCII-dash grep)
6. Report cap + explicit don'ts (no commit/deploy/DB writes unless stated)

## Review discipline

Review seams, not surfaces: grep the contract joints (header names, param shapes, grants)
instead of re-reading whole files. Read deviation notes first - they carry the real-world facts
the brief got wrong. Keep parallel workstreams in disjoint directories.

## Never economize on

Security-adjacent changes, anything crossing more than two components, deploy decisions, and
debugging where the symptom lies.
